eason":2022,"team":"IND","distance":0.8633,"shared":21,"play_caller":"Frank Reich"},{"season":2024,"team":"IND","distance":0.8812,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Shane Steichen"},{"season":2022,"team":"NYG","distance":0.9257,"shared":21,"play_caller":"Mike Kafka"}],"defense":[{"season":2025,"team":"BUF","distance":0.6307,"shared":22,"play_caller":null},{"season":2019,"team":"NO","distance":0.6511,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Dennis Allen"},{"season":2024,"team":"CIN","distance":0.6645,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Lou Anarumo"},{"season":2022,"team":"CIN","distance":0.6695,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Lou Anarumo"},{"season":2023,"team":"ATL","distance":0.6704,"shared":22,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Eagles went 11-6 in 2023 and outscored opponents 433 to 428. The offense carried the team, ranking 8th in EPA per play while the defense finished 29th in EPA allowed. What set them apart was the highest shotgun rate and a 2-Man rate that was among the highest in the league.","offense":"The Eagles had one of the most efficient offenses in the league, ranking in the top eight in four of the five main efficiency measures. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Schematically, they stood out for the highest shotgun rate and the lowest under center rate in the league. Jalen Hurts ran the passing game and leaned heavily on A.J. Brown, who drew 30% of the targets.","defense":"The Eagles had one of the worst defenses in the league, ranking 26th or worse in four of the five main efficiency measures. Even its strongest area, explosive play rate allowed, only ranked 19th, and it was 30th in EPA allowed per rush. Haason Reddick was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Jalen Carter, who had 6 sacks while playing only 51% of the snaps, after not being a regular on this defense last season. Schematically, they had a 2-Man rate that was among the highest in the league.","special":"The Eagles were one of the most aggressive teams on fourth down, going for it on 52% of their short-yardage chances in normal situations. Their kickers made 94% of field goals, which was one of the best in the league. Their salary cap was spread across positions in a fairly typical way. Their offensive line was among the oldest in the league.","players":"Jalen Hurts played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 3,858 yards. A.J. Brown was the clear top target with 30% of the looks and 1,456 receiving yards. D'Andre Swift handled most of the work in the backfield with 66% of the carries. On defense, Haason Reddick led the team with 11 sacks and Reed Blankenship picked off 3 passes.","coaching":"This staff is not in the coaching data, so the comparisons below are based on scheme alone. Their offense looked most like the 2025 Giants under Mike Kafka and the 2024 Commanders under Kliff Kingsbury. Their defense looked most like the 2025 Bills and the 2019 Saints under Dennis Allen.","trends":"The offense went from 3rd in EPA per play in 2022 to 8th in 2023, a modest decline. The defense moved from 5th in EPA allowed per play in 2022 to 29th in 2023, a big step back. The biggest change in approach was Cover 2 rate, which went from 5% to 17%.","notes":"Every data source is available for 2023. This season uses the newer formation labels, which group singleback, I formation and empty sets together as under center, so those older formation rates are not shown."}}